Table 6
 presents the physico and chemical properties of the dopants, highlighting that only Mg2+ and Zn2+ possess ionic radio and electronegativity values comparable to Ca2+. This similarity facilitates their incorporation into the C3S structure via substitutional solid solution mechanisms. In contrast, Ti4+ has significantly smaller ionic dimensions and different chemical characteristics, which favors the formation of interstitial solid solutions (Fig. 6
). This structural behavior is corroborated by the observed amounts of free CaO (f-CaO) in the doped samples: phases doped with MgO exhibit higher f-CaO content compared to those doped with TiO2 (Table 3), suggesting more substantial lattice disruption and limited incorporation.

This work studied the effect of doping on the reactivity of C3S and its polymorphs, using different techniques and analyzing the properties in the anhydrous and hydrated state. The results indicate:

•

The types and contents of dopants used stabilized the T3 and M1 polymorphs at room temperature;


•

The type of solid solution formed directly influences the growth of C3S crystals, affecting surface properties and average crystallite size. Substitutional solid solutions tend to generate larger defects with vacancies to relieve substitution stresses, and interstitial solutions can promote grain growth arrest, which will impact average crystallite size;


•

The MgO and ZnO dopants, at both doping contents, caused changes in the volume of the unit cell, displacement of the chemical bonds, and orientation in the [SiO]4
4− silicon tetrahedrons;


•

C3S doped with MgO showed greater reactivity in the hydration process for both contents when compared to the pure phase and phases doped with ZnO and TiO2;


•

The results showed that the doping technique generates a catalytic effect in the hydration process of C3S, with an increase in the energy released and reaction time for the contents of 0.5 wt% and 1.0 wt% MgO, and a reduction in the energy released in the phases doped with ZnO and TiO2, regardless of the stabilized polymorph. With the increase in content to 4.0 wt% and 8.0 wt%, a physical barrier is formed, which hinders the diffusion process, making it challenging to form C-S-H. Therefore, the reactivity of C3S cannot be evaluated considering only the forming polymorph.
